  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a3/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_7.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The Vulture becomes the best behaved prisoner during his months behind bars, allowing him to be trusted with use of the machine shop.&nbsp; He eventually escapes from prison after fashioning a new flying device that he uses to soar over the grounds' wall.\n\nAt school, Peter Parker hears about the Vulture's escape and feigns sickness so he can go home, change to Spider-Man, and be ready for him.&nbsp; The Vulture, now refit with \"new modified wings,\" tests his maneuverability by easily evading a police helicopter, before robbing a jewelry showroom, all before finally running into Spider-Man.&nbsp; The hero believes his anti-magnetic inverter (that he built in [LINK:1963:ASM_2_asm1|ASM #2|2026-07-31]) will work once again, so the Vulture allows him to get close enough to use the device, then <i>pretends</i> that he's spiraling to the ground, only to turn and strike Spider-Man as he reaches the edge of a rooftop.\n\nA short struggle ensues and the surprised Spider-Man falls to a rooftop, seemingly defeated, causing the Vulture to gloat and fly off.&nbsp; But Peter wakes up and finds his arm badly sprained from the impact.&nbsp; Aunt May takes him to a doctor, who says his arm \"will be fine in a few weeks!\"&nbsp; Peter takes a lot of ribbing at school, of course, while the Vulture hides out in an abandoned farm silo on Staten Island.&nbsp; His next heist will be the <i>Daily Bugle</i> which has \"one of the biggest payrolls in New York.\"&nbsp; He's after the \"hundreds of nice, fat, juicy pay envelopes.\"\n\nPeter goes to the Bugle to sell Jonah his latest photo, only to have the Vulture show up in person and demand the entire payroll.&nbsp; Jameson is so tight with money that he actually <i>argues</i> with the Vulture over it, even while being held at gunpoint!&nbsp; Peter slips away, manages to change to Spider-Man, and web up his bad arm enough for him to battle the Vulture.&nbsp; The two engage in battle right in the middle of the Daily Bugle offices, with Jameson hurling out threats and pleading for them to stop before they wreck the place!\n\nThey descend into the press room and then out of the building, where the Vulture hauls Spider-Man way up into the air, preparing to drop him before Spider-Man webs up his wings, jamming them up, and the pair begin to fall.&nbsp; The Vulture pleads for his life, and Spider-Man whips up a web parachute.&nbsp; Once low enough to reach the buildings, he leaves the Vulture alone to descend slowly into the hands of the waiting police below.\n\nBut as Spider-Man prepares to swing away, Jameson shouts out how he holds him personally responsible and won't rest until he finds out who he is.&nbsp; Spider-Man webs his mouth shut.&nbsp; \"What have I got to lose?&nbsp; You couldn't hate me any more than you do <i>now</i>!\"&nbsp; He heads back into the Daily Bugle to change back into Peter Parker and finds Betty Brant hiding behind a desk and joins her, claiming he was hiding in a closet.\n\nBetty and Peter share a moment together, Peter complimenting her perfume.&nbsp; Betty is thrilled with the romantic undertones, and the two of them snuggle up together and enjoy each other's company, all while Jameson races back and forth through the office, unable to speak a word... \"for at least an hour!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | VultureEscape | width=13%] The Vulture escaping prison by becoming the \"best behaved prisoner in the place\" is about par for the course in these early 1960s stories, where long prison sentences rarely prevent villains from returning after only a handful of issues.\n[BR]\n[BR] And so it is no surprise when, by page 3, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=the Vulture is on the loose again!]\n\nRight off the bat, this story looked like it was going to amount to nothing more than the next entry in the long-running series of [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=\"The Return of the... <i>fill-in-the-blank</i>\"] -- immediately causing my hackles to rise, certain that this was going to be as mundane as most of those others had been thus far.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Boy, was I pleasantly surprised!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | GleefulVulture | width=10%] Just like he did the <i>first</i> time the Vulture appeared, Ditko hits a home run with the panel that zooms in on the villain's face.&nbsp; The Vulture almost <i>gleefully</i> flies toward Spider-Man, who still believes that his anti-magnetic inverter device will work... and even prepares to snap photos of his easy victory for the Daily Bugle.\n\nBut by pretending that Spider-Man's device still works to thwart his magnetic wings, the Vulture takes Spider-Man unawares (who is still figuring out his complicated spider sense and, at this time, doesn't even seem to <i>trust</i> it).&nbsp; This sends the surprised hero crashing down onto a rooftop at the top of page 9, where he twists around just enough for his arm to take the brunt of the impact.\n\n[ASSET: left | BrokenArm | width=12%] [ASSET: right | FewWeeks | width=12%] This result?&nbsp; Spider-Man/Peter Parker is forced to nurse an injured arm for the rest of the magazine, and you have to give Stan and Steve major credit here.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=All of this largely low-key action has consumed about half the book without anyone really noticing.]\n[BR]\n[BR] The action was smooth and well-calculated!\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAfter showing Peter Parker taking major abuse at school due to his hurt arm, the co-creators deliver another great sequence showing a confident Vulture using his old hideout, which is \"an innocent-looking abandoned farm silo on Staten Island, not far from the heart of Manhattan\" (the city name spelled <i>correctly</i> this time, unlike the first time it was portrayed back in [LINK:1963:ASM_2_asm1|ASM #2|2026-07-31]).\n[ASSET: center | VulturePlots | width=67%]\n\nYou just have to <i>love </i>the way Ditko draws the supremely-confident pose on the part of the Vulture -- this guy is <i>obviously</i> at ease!\n\n[ASSET: left | AskASillyQuestion | width=20%] But more to the point, the Vulture decides to go to the very place Peter is going, which is to the offices of the <i>Daily Bugle</i> and try to make money from his photos, stopping on the way to see Betty, who asks him about his injury.\n[BR]\n[BR] And I can't imagine <i>anyone</i> not enjoying their brief interaction.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>Another </i>stupendous moment, again punctuated by <i>perfect </i>artwork.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | SkinflintJonah | width=11%] But it was <i>nothing</i> compared to the hilarious reaction of Jameson arguing with the Vulture, who was holding him at [SPAN: severity=error; text=gunpoint], <i>pleading</i> with him not to steal his payroll money.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even Peter Parker can't believe what he's seeing, but he quietly slips aside and becomes Spider-Man.\n\nWhat follows was a four-page battle inside of the <i>Daily Bugle</i> offices, with J. Jonah Jameson either pleading for his life or begging the combatants <i>not</i> to destroy everything in sight.&nbsp; It was indeed comical to hear Jameson calling out things like:\n[HANG] \"Spider-Man!&nbsp; <i>Look out</i>, you fool!&nbsp; I'll be <i>hurt</i>!\"\n[HANG] \"You two will <i>wreck</i> the place!\"\n[HANG] \"<i>My ledgers!&nbsp; My files!! </i>Look what you're <i>doing</i>!\" [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=with Spider-Man responding \"Aw, go slide down a barbed-wire fence!\"]\n\nYou can only imagine his horror when the two battled above his vast printing press machines.\n\nBut eventually, the fight proceeded out of the building and into the sky above the city, and as you might expect, Spider-Man outwits his opponent and lowers him into the hands of the police.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Pretty run of the mill stuff...]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | WebUpMouth | width=9%] [SPAN: severity=success; text=That's why the final flourish was so brilliant], showing Jameson yapping at Spider-Man and  blaming him for all the damage caused, which finally led the hero to snap and web the blowhard's mouth closed.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=After all, Jameson couldn't <i>possibly </i>hate him any more than he already does, <i>right</i>?]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=(This is great stuff!)]\n\nPeter finds Betty, still hiding behind a desk inside, and the two share a private laugh as Jameson rants and raves around the office (albeit <i>wordlessly</i>).\n[ASSET: center | TheEnd | width=67%]\n\nIt was the <i>perfect</i> end to the case.\n[HANG] The villain is recaptured.\n[HANG] Jameson is silenced.\n[HANG] Betty and Peter grow closer together.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nStill...I <i>do</i> have to wonder [SPAN: severity=warning; text=what Betty is thinking.]&nbsp; She's a professional girl, presumably already a high-school graduate, possibly having spent at least one year in some sort of secretarial training to become qualified enough to be the personal assistant of the publisher of a major New York City newspaper, [DECODER_RING: For some perspective on what kind of qualifications this requires, look at all the trouble Jameson later had <i>replacing</i> Brant in issues in the vicinity of 157-159] and yet she's flirting with Peter Parker, who's right-around 16 years old!\n\nShe <i>has</i> to be a couple of years <i>older</i> than him, perhaps even more.&nbsp; I find it hard to believe that she would not have any other interested guys her own age.&nbsp; The tale suffers from this in my opinion, and even with the previous foreshadowing an issue or two earlier, it still comes across as surprising.\n\nIn its defense however, let's chalk this up to different times -- employees received fat, juicy pay envelopes filled with cash at the end of the work week, and apparently, young professional girls flirted with high school guys.\n\nI was only a year old when this story was published, so who am I to say if this was or wasn't the way things were?&nbsp; It's not fair to judge a story out of the context of its day, so while my 21<sup>st</sup> century eyes might widen at these aspects and cause me to raise questions, I will keep trying for the most part <i>not</i> to cast judgment.\n\nIt's the curse of having hazy \"future knowledge,\" being from the 21<sup>st</sup> century as I am, reading these stories from inside [PAGE: scenario].&nbsp; Inevitably, something causes my eyebrows to rise!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis issue was a major improvement over the last one, bringing back a former villain for the first time, and taking full advantage of the wealth of the supporting cast.&nbsp; The story itself wasn't stupendous, but it flowed so smoothly, that the fact that the villain wasn't first-rate wasn't at all distracting.\n\nThe sight of Jameson arguing with the Vulture the prospect of having his payroll cash robbed -- <i>classic</i> and so in-character for him -- was <b>laugh-out-loud hilarious</b>, as was his later interaction with Spider-Man.&nbsp; Having the hero web up his mouth was ten times better than the nastygram note Spider-Man left for him at the end of <i>last </i>month's tale, and a sure sign that Stan and Steve seemed to be fully focused and on the same page.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=The art and dialogue were superb.]\n\nAs a villain, the Vulture will never approach the power level of a Dr. Octopus or a Sandman, [DECODER_RING: Or the still-to-come and even deadlier foes of Spider-Man such as the Green Goblin or Kingpin, to name just a couple...] but being only the 7<sup>th</sup> issue and having just <i>featured</i> the first two aforementioned villains, Marvel used what they had well.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Furthermore, it was noted on the splash page that readers were actually <i>clamoring</i> to see more of the Vulture, so his reuse can be forgiven in that regard.]\n\nI could perhaps have done without Peter's ever-jeering peers, deriding him for having an injured arm.&nbsp; I don't blame Peter at all for being livid with anger.&nbsp; No matter how much success he has as Spider-Man (and even <i>that</i> is a matter of opinion), he always ends up the butt of their jokes, constantly berated in their midst.&nbsp; It's perhaps why he's fascinated with Betty Brant, someone who's more mature, has a job, and is off on her own.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=(Whether <i>Betty</i> should be involved with a high school student is something I already covered above!)]\n\nDespite the \"<b>The Return of the <i>fill-in-the-blank</i></b>\" motif (with the Vulture filling in this month's blank), the story was crafted well, the humor sharp, the irony evident in the ongoing Peter Parker/Spider-Man interaction, and <i>hey</i>... for once, Peter gets the girl!\n\nIt wasn't a masterpiece, but it was well worth the cover price!",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * Page 4 establishes that Peter and his aunt live in a \"quiet residential neighborhood in Forest Hills.\"\n\n[HANG0] * On page 10, Peter notes that, due to his injured arm, \"it took almost a [SPAN: severity=info; text=half hour] to change duds!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] Yet on page 21, after his battle with the Vulture is over, at the point where he changes back to Peter Parker inside the <i>Daily Bugle</i> building, the narration states:&nbsp; \"And [SPAN: severity=info; text=within seconds], the Amazing <i>Spider-Man</i> vanishes, to be replaced by a sling-wearing, innocent-looking Peter Parker!\n[BR]\n[BR] <i>Granted</i> that the second battle took place the day <i>after</i> his original injury and that his spider powers likely helped accelerate his healing, but that is <i>still</i> a fast turnaround!"
  ],
  "letters_page": "Paul Gambaccini's letter contained some funny comments about Stan's self-promotion:&nbsp; \"And it's not the same old Stan Lee either - whose claims of 'We're superior!&nbsp; We're different!&nbsp; We're the best!&nbsp; We're too great for words!' were trying to promote his own work (sort of like most of your competitors).\"\n\nHowever, what might be the most far-reaching of his comments was what followed:&nbsp; \"I don't know if their stuff has deteriorated or whether you have improved that much, but the competition now seems like [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=echh!]\"\n\nEven more amazing was the response:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=We appreciate your praise, Paul, but feel we must pause for a serious word to you, and all of fandom.&nbsp; We don't feel it's fair for you to knock any other group of magazines while boosting ours.&nbsp; After all, it's just a matter of taste -- and there are probably many fans who prefer our competitors' mags to ours.&nbsp; There are many fine talents producing well-written and well-drawn stories at other houses besides MARVEL.&nbsp; While we admit we try to create the greatest comics possible, we feel it isn't proper to comment about mags other than our own on these pages.&nbsp; Okay?]\n\nThis serious and respectful response is eye-opening in the light of what later transitions to the open mocking of [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Brand Echh] by none other than Stan himself.&nbsp; However, we have to remember that it's still early in Marvel's existence, and the small staff size and minimal output at the time, together with no established record of long-term success, may well have contributed to this [SPAN: severity=info; text=early, measured, and <i>far</i> more cautionary stance.]",
  "decoder_ring": "Years later, the ending of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> 157 (ASM #157) has Spider-Man falling down into pasturelands where there are no buildings to grab hold of, and he cries out [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"Face it hero -- this time you've bought the farm!\"] (a scene I remember quite vividly because I didn't get my hands on the next issue for a <b><i>very long</i></b> time...)\n\nObviously, in the moment, he seems to have forgotten about the web parachute he created years earlier as part of <i>this</i> issue!",
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}